{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And they told Moses, etc.</b> From here it would seem that he had not informed them beforehand of the word of HaShem. And it is difficult: how could he, heaven forfend, suppress his prophecy? And our Rabbis taught us (Sanhedrin 89a) the law of a prophet who suppresses his prophecy, etc. — heaven forfend! And all the more so regarding the communication of a commandment for Israel to fulfill the will of the King, in which there are two aspects: suppressing prophecy and uprooting the commandment. And one cannot say that he, heaven forfend, forgot the commandment of HaShem — if so, we must be concerned for him as well that he forgot what he was commanded and was still in a state of having forgotten, heaven forfend. Moreover, there was no reason for him to delay any amount of time for forgetfulness, and immediately and at once he should have told his prophecy. And also, a difficulty — before our God: why did HaShem not remind him when He knew that he had forgotten? At that very moment He should have reminded him and not left him in a state of forgetting for seven days, until he was reminded on his own." "<b>Truly,</b> Moses our teacher had his reason and his proof with him, and with the sweetness of his expression he gave an answer like poured molten mirror (Job 37:18), in his saying <b>״This is that which HaShem spoke: a solemn rest, a holy sabbath,״</b> etc. Here, in the pleasantness of his words, he omitted the word ״לֵאמֹר,״ hinting that HaShem did not speak to him לֵאמֹר to them, and therefore the matter is utterly not as you would say, as they said, of blessed memory (Yoma 4a), and we wrote them in the verse (Exodus 16:11): ״וַיְדַבֵּר, etc., שָׁמַעְתִּי,״ etc. And this is the reason he did not inform them beforehand. And the faithful witness to Moses' words — HaShem Himself attests to it, in the formulation of the prophecy when He said to him (Exodus 16:5): ״וְהָיָה בַּיּוֹם הַשִּׁשִּׁי,״ etc. You will find that HaShem did not say to him there ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ nor the word ״לֵאמֹר.״ Behold, the matter was sealed before him, that he had no commission regarding this commandment to Israel. And that is what he said — ״הוּא״ — after the fact, when they gathered a double portion of bread and came before him, he revealed the matter to them and told them that it had been with him only by way of a secret — the things were handed over to him, and not to say them." "<b>And there is</b> for us to pay attention according to this: who gave him permission to say to them all the commandments stated in His words, concerning which it was not said to him ״דַּבֵּר״ nor ״לֵאמֹר״?" "<b>And it appears</b> that Moses acted with the discernment of a sage as to why HaShem did not want him to say the things to Israel, and he understood that the reason is that HaShem desired to plant in them the planting of faith and the vindication of the commandment of Shabbat, so that its acceptance and its knowledge would come from El Elyon Himself — not through an agent at first, but rather when they would go out to gather according to their usual daily measure and they would measure and find double the daily portion, they would see tangible and intelligible proof that HaShem is commanding them not to exert themselves on the day of Shabbat, and provides for them from the sixth day the need of the seventh day. In this way this commandment would be received by them with an added measure of acceptance of the will and great awe of transgressing it, since its greatness is great, and as they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 25:11): Shabbat is weighed against the entire Torah. Therefore Moses did not command them beforehand regarding Shabbat, so that they would not plan to increase the gathering and would gather according to the daily rule, and the miracle of finding double would be performed for them — this is nothing but the deed of Elohim, which He designed for them to observe Shabbat." "And if you should say: after all, Moses would in the end command them regarding Shabbat, and say to them that they would gather only the usual measure in order to recognize the thing — it is not in keeping with [His] pedagogy to make them understand that a miracle is needed to vindicate Moses' words; therefore HaShem acted wisely." "<b>And further</b>, if He had transmitted the commandments through Moses, there would still be room to err, in the manner of what they said in Menachot, chapter Rabbi Yishmael (Menachot 65a), when Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai engaged the accursed Baytusin and said to them: ״From where do you derive that Shavuot is after the Sabbath?״ And an elder among them pecked back at him and said: ״Moses loved Israel and knew that Shavuot is a single day, so he established it after the Sabbath so that they would enjoy two days״ — end quote. And the Tosafot wrote there, and this is their language: he established it, and the Holy One, blessed be He, agreed through him — end quote. You see then that there are those who err, for even when a commandment is from the mouth of Moses the Holy One, blessed be He, may agree through him; if so, there is a difference between having prior knowledge of the commandment through the testing of the Supernal El and not having it. Therefore HaShem did not command Moses to say this thing to Israel, and from the moment the thought that Elohim conceived went forth and Israel recognized the matter first, Moses said to them <b>it is what YHWH spoke</b> to me — <b>a sabbath of solemn rest, a holy Sabbath.</b>" "And if you say: why did the recognition not suffice to attain knowledge of the Sabbath through it, so that Moses needed to say the matter to them? Without the words of Moses they would err and say perhaps the descent of the manna has ended, and for this reason HaShem gave it to them not in the usual order. Therefore Moses said to them <b>it is what He spoke</b> etc., and for no other reason; and the matter will be definitively recognized by them when the manna descends one day after the Sabbath. And Moses was compelled to say the matter to them, which is a prohibition, so that they would not stumble on that Sabbath and go out — except that he did not state the warning to them explicitly, as I will explain in the verse ״עַד אָנָה מֵאַנְתֶם״ (Exodus 16:28)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:18.094408295Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}